WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.522 --> 00:00:03.210
When I was 24 years old,

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:07.520
I saw the UFC on a pay-per-view on TV.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:11.431
And I was so overwhelmed by
the athleticism, the skill.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:20.810
And then at the end of the
fight, the combatants hug,

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:24.200
they shook hands, and I
just fell in love with it.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.230
Part of UFC's appeal is it doesn't

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:33.230
look like a balletic display
of marshal talent and mastery,

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:36.824
as much as it looks like a bar fight.

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:47.010
We choose to do this
because we love the sport,

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:48.280
but it is a business.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.130
And if you do not treat
it like a business,

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:53.130
I guarantee you that the promoter is.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:54.650
And you will be left broken,

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:57.080
broken at the end of your career.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:59.750
Each of us have an individual story,

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:01.040
but as with anything,

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.960
you take enough of
these individual stories

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:06.420
and you see not only crossover,
but intersectionality

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.470
A group of former UFC fighters

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:10.120
that are suing the UFC

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:13.460
scored a key legal victory on Thursday

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:15.350
when a federal judge said he would grant

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:18.224
their lawsuit class-action status.

00:01:18.224 --> 00:01:21.800
There have been in fights
in every major US sport

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:25.640
about workers' power in the labor market.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.640
I don't think there's a guy in the sport,

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:31.280
from the guy who just got his first fight,

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.110
all the way up to the Conor McGregor's,

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:36.060
that is pleased with the
way the UFC treats fighters.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.890
The way we've been strong-armed,
the way we've been coerced.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:42.970
Fights over unionization in some sports,

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:47.160
fights over free agency in baseball.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:50.200
And in boxing, the Mohammad Ali Act,

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:53.250
which restricts the
kinds of contract terms

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:56.120
that can be imposed on boxers.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:57.570
The fighters who brought this lawsuit

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:01.520
see it as the continuation
of a tradition in sports

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:05.520
of athletes fighting to have agency

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.020
and power in the terms of their work,

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:10.523
and who they do it for.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:19.520
Mixed martial arts used
to be pretty culturally

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:22.430
and legally fringe in the United States.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:25.100
It was banned by most states.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:28.333
John McCain had famously
called it human cockfighting.

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:34.540
There are some rules in the
UFC, but to a large extent

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:39.180
all sorts of body parts,
and tactics, and attacks

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:44.130
are on the table, and people
get seriously injured at times.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.180
My nose has been broken multiple times.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:48.000
I had my right pectoral torn off,

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.650
had to get that reattached.

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:51.470
I've had my jaw broken in two places.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:52.613
I've had my neck broken.

00:02:52.613 --> 00:02:56.310
I had my left ankle broken,
had to get that repaired.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:59.270
I've had a torn right
labrum in my shoulder.

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:00.380
He broke my eyeball.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.790
I mean, the bone that holds up my eyeball.

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:06.450
I've had my left eye
orbital fracture twice,

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:07.570
where my I caved in.

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:08.800
I was swallowing my own blood.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:11.600
And I knew, don't blow your
nose or both eyes will blow.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:16.710
At the time that UFC was
bought for just $2 million

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:22.670
by the former boxing coach,
Dana White, and a casino owner

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:26.370
he'd been friends with, Lorenzo Fertitta,

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:30.370
and his brother, Frank
Fertitta, UFC was struggling,

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:33.480
as were other mixed
martial arts promoters.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.850
It was pretty much a fixer upper.

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:38.870
The money that they spent
to actually purchase it

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:41.560
for $2 million was much
less than the money

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:46.170
they quickly had to sink into
it in order to revitalize it.

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:49.100
They pulled off a multi-pronged strategy

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:51.540
that was quite successful.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:54.610
First, they did what White has referred to

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:57.310
as running towards regulation.

00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:01.850
They found ways to make
compromises with state regulators

00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:05.530
so that the sport would not be illegal.

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:08.960
And at the same time,
they also popularized it.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.440
They made gambles like paying

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.920
the production costs themselves

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.477
for the first season of a reality TV show,

00:04:16.477 --> 00:04:18.720
"The Ultimate Fighter" on Spike TV.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.027
It's all over, it is all over!

00:04:21.878 --> 00:04:25.880
Diego Sanchez is the ultimate fighter!

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:29.600
UFC, under Dana White,
had a gift for weaving

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.780
compelling personal
narratives and rivalry.

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:34.940
People throwing shoes,

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:37.707
a guy coming to the octagon with

00:04:37.707 --> 00:04:39.300
"Who Let the Dogs Out" playing

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:42.286
while he was dragging
another guy on a leash.

00:04:42.286 --> 00:04:43.947
♪ Who let the dogs out? ♪

00:04:43.947 --> 00:04:46.250
♪ Woof, woof, woof, woof ♪

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:48.160
My first contract with the UFC

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:49.750
is kind of an interesting story,

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:51.927
because here I am on this reality show,

00:04:51.927 --> 00:04:53.460
"The Ultimate Fighter,"

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:56.690
and they started to realize
how big of a stepping stone

00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:58.830
this was going to be for the UFC.

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:00.950
Sat us all down individually,

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:03.240
put a contract in front of us and said,

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:05.900
hey, we realize that
this is gonna be huge.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:08.077
And we really wanna have you all a part of

00:05:08.077 --> 00:05:10.670
the UFC family moving forward.

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:12.630
So we wanna go ahead and
get this out of the way.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:14.100
Just sign this simple contract

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:17.220
and we'll be able to
move forward as one unit.

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:19.800
But as I'm looking at this thick contract

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.690
and wondering what I should do,

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:24.810
Dana White looks at me
and says, don't worry.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.160
If this thing takes off
we'll take care of you.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:32.890
The fighters would say, at
the heart of UFC's power

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:35.993
is the contracts that
it signs with fighters.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:39.150
Before boxing had competition

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:42.200
boxing used to be dominated
by a few dominant promoters.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:44.810
And the contracts were
ported over from boxing.

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:46.670
And the contracts are basically designed

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:49.110
to control every aspect
of a fighter's career.

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:50.250
They're long-term.

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:52.450
They last multiple fights.

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:55.600
Fighters, under these contracts,

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:00.600
are bound to fight only for
UFC for a period of time.

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:05.060
And UFC can extend that time

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:07.590
if a fighter declines a match,

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:10.400
or if a fighter gets injured.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:12.360
They control who the fighter fights,

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:14.180
they control when the fighter fights.

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:16.760
And then they have a
number of different clauses

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:19.440
that make it very difficult for a fighter

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:21.420
to ever break free from the UFC

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:24.380
if that fighter ever did
want to become a free agent.

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:26.420
Contracts are so one-sided,

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:30.270
there really is no negotiation
room for any of the things,

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:33.330
especially as you're coming
up, and as their stranglehold

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:36.593
on the sport of MMA got so much stronger.

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:41.310
Part of what's at the
heart of this lawsuit

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:45.000
is the ways that UFC successfully

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:48.640
took other competitors off the board.

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:51.390
The UFC has such a stranglehold

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:52.730
on mixed martial arts.

00:06:52.730 --> 00:06:54.873
And as they were buying up
so much of the competition,

00:06:54.873 --> 00:06:59.873
shutting them all down, they
really became the only option

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.230
for anybody that wanted
to do this professionally.

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:06.870
If you follow Dana White's Twitter,

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:09.120
or watch him on YouTube,

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:12.210
you could've seen Dana
White calling himself

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:14.830
the Grim Reaper, and bragging about

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:19.830
how he has defeated, or
derailed, or acquired

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:22.350
one after another company

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:24.943
that tried to compete with the UFC.

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:30.950
In 2011, UFC acquired
Strikeforce, which at the time

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:35.128
was the most prominent
rival still standing.

00:07:35.128 --> 00:07:37.777
Cung Le is unbelievable.

00:07:37.777 --> 00:07:38.610
It was a great fight.

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:39.820
Let's hear it for them one more time.

00:07:39.820 --> 00:07:42.290
Scott Smith and Cung Le!

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:46.090
The CEO of Strikeforce, Scott Coker,

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:50.220
has said in the past that he
saw himself like Luke Skywalker

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:54.130
up against the Death Star,
trying to compete with UFC.

00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:57.820
Eventually, when Strikeforce
was bought out by UFC,

00:07:57.820 --> 00:07:59.447
my only option was go to UFC

00:07:59.447 --> 00:08:01.560
'cause they had the rest of my contract.

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:04.880
Now I'm under a contract where it's only

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:07.440
an 18 month contract with six fights.

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:10.170
But five years later, I'm
still stuck in the contract.

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:11.050
Dana White, president of--

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:15.240
The month before UFC's
parent company, Zuffa,

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:18.670
completed its acquisition of Strikeforce,

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:22.090
a UFC executive sent an email.

00:08:22.090 --> 00:08:25.707
And the subject line of
that email was, We Own MMA.

00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:29.870
And the UFC has admitted in documents

00:08:29.870 --> 00:08:32.270
that have now become
public, that they've almost

00:08:32.270 --> 00:08:34.630
never lost a fighter
that they wanted to keep.

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:38.480
In internal UFC emails we now know

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:41.980
the company referred to what it was doing

00:08:41.980 --> 00:08:45.870
as choking off oxygen to the competition.

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:50.480
The most important
oxygen that UFC allegedly

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:55.170
ran afoul of the law by
cutting off to its competitors,

00:08:55.170 --> 00:08:57.200
is the fighters themselves.

00:08:57.200 --> 00:08:58.640
And not only did they make it clear to us,

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:00.820
they made it clear to the
whole mixed martial arts world.

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:02.220
There is no negotiating.

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:05.580
These are our very strict,
never-ending contracts.

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:07.920
And that's the case to this day.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:10.240
UFC just says, oh, you wanna fight for us?

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:11.120
You sign here.

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:12.040
If you don't want to,

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:14.730
we've got 20 other guys
that'll take your spot.

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:18.020
They have 70, 80, 90% of the top fighters

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:19.620
under exclusive contract.

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:21.960
Meaning that those
fighters are not available

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:24.170
to any other MMA promoter.

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:26.710
And fighters, even if
they wiggled their way out

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:30.770
of the very restrictive contracts
that UFC imposed on them,

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:35.570
didn't have another professional
mixed martial arts option

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:38.170
because there weren't robust rivals

00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:40.490
to go sell their work to.

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:42.730
On Tuesday afternoon,
a class-action lawsuit

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:45.190
was filed in US district court in San Jose

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:48.420
against the Zuffa LLC with
fighters Cung Le, Nate Quarry,

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:50.500
and Jon Fitch attached to the suit.

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:53.580
Rampage Jackson came to
me, he says, hey Cung,

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:54.500
I know this lawyer.

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:57.982
He will help get some
fairness for the fighters

00:09:57.982 --> 00:10:00.080
in the marketplace.

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:03.460
And I decided I wanted to step up

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:05.703
for all the fighters
that are being affected.

00:10:05.703 --> 00:10:10.470
At the end of 2014, more
than six years ago now,

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:13.990
a group of now all former fighters

00:10:13.990 --> 00:10:18.990
filed a anti-trust class-action
lawsuit against UFC.

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:23.060
And in broad terms, the
case is an anti-trust case,

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:24.640
where we're claiming that the UFC

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:27.270
is both a monopoly and a monopsony.

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:30.350
Usually when we talk about monopoly,

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:34.020
we're talking about the
power that a company has

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:37.560
when it becomes the only
one selling something.

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:40.820
Monopsony is about the power of being

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:43.350
a monopoly purchaser of something,

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:48.350
and particularly a monopoly
purchaser of labor.

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:52.720
A key argument in this
lawsuit has been about,

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:56.760
as UFC succeeds, and its revenue soars,

00:10:56.760 --> 00:11:01.760
how much should we expect
that, absent illegal conduct,

00:11:02.270 --> 00:11:05.460
the fighters pay would soar as well?

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:08.380
In UFC, year after year,

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:09.910
the share of the company's revenue

00:11:09.910 --> 00:11:12.740
that goes to actually paying the workers

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:17.740
doing the fighting has
hovered around or below 20%.

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:20.270
And what this analysis shows

00:11:20.270 --> 00:11:23.940
through complex statistical
regression modeling

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:27.700
is that if the UFC was competitive,

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:31.010
and did not allegedly
violate the anti-trust laws,

00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:35.090
it would pay its fighters 50% or more

00:11:35.090 --> 00:11:37.660
of the revenues it's
generated, just like baseball,

00:11:37.660 --> 00:11:41.580
just like hockey, just
like basketball, boxing.

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:44.840
If instead of paying 20%
of the revenues generated

00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:48.320
it paid 50% or more of
the revenues generated,

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:52.130
the fighters would have
made $1.6 billion more

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:56.820
during the period that we
measured, 2010 to 2017.

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:00.040
Sometimes when you write about allegations

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:03.830
against companies, and
particularly about lawsuits

00:12:03.830 --> 00:12:07.180
against companies, those companies

00:12:07.180 --> 00:12:10.140
don't wanna say a whole lot about them,

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:13.640
or are pretty dismissive
of the significance

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:15.260
of litigation against them.

00:12:15.260 --> 00:12:18.170
UFC, in their comments to me,

00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:20.940
has been pretty feisty about this.

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:23.530
Saying that they see this lawsuit actually

00:12:23.530 --> 00:12:26.330
as a threat to the ability of businesses

00:12:26.330 --> 00:12:28.690
to take risks and succeed.

00:12:28.690 --> 00:12:32.470
And that they don't wanna roll over,

00:12:32.470 --> 00:12:35.253
that they want to fight it,
and they want to defeat it.

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:40.910
The fighters I talked to
described having side jobs,

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:45.480
being reliant on UFC in part because,

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:49.870
after taxes and expenses, they weren't

00:12:49.870 --> 00:12:53.640
making enough money to
feel financially secure.

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:56.260
The overview of my career
from a financial perspective

00:12:56.260 --> 00:13:00.100
is something I've had a
challenge in admitting.

00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:02.760
I can tell you now that I lived
in my mom's detached garage

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:03.950
the entire time I was fighting,

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:07.010
and I had a second job as
a bouncer and a bartender,

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:10.083
a third job as a personal
trainer, just to make ends meet.

00:13:10.990 --> 00:13:14.130
Nate Quarry told me that
over his time in UFC

00:13:14.130 --> 00:13:19.080
he fought fights while he was
tens of thousands of dollars

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:21.550
in debt coming into the fight.

00:13:21.550 --> 00:13:25.680
It gets so disheartening
because I've seen fights

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:27.960
that are called the fight of the year.

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:30.550
This is just this brutal war.

00:13:30.550 --> 00:13:33.100
And they battled for
four and a half rounds

00:13:33.100 --> 00:13:35.780
until one guy's body just gave up.

00:13:35.780 --> 00:13:36.800
And then you find out, oh,

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:39.193
that guy made $60,000 for that fight.

00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:44.240
In one of the hearings in this lawsuit,

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:47.080
UFC's attorney argued that

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:49.270
if the fighters were really the product,

00:13:49.270 --> 00:13:52.640
if all that UFC were
selling were the fighters,

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:54.850
then the fighters
wouldn't need UFC at all.

00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:59.850
That a fighter is not
themselves the UFC product,

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:03.840
any more than an Apple engineer

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:06.680
who makes a cool iPhone is the product

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:08.370
that Apple is selling.

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:11.630
We the fighters make
this whole thing happen.

00:14:11.630 --> 00:14:13.280
And it's been driven by Chuck Liddell,

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:14.700
it's been driven by Randy Couture,

00:14:14.700 --> 00:14:15.743
it's been driven by Conor McGregor,

00:14:15.743 --> 00:14:17.270
it's been driven by Brock Lesnar.

00:14:17.270 --> 00:14:19.420
It's been driven by all
the greats that have walked

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:22.010
before us and that walk
this path right now.

00:14:22.010 --> 00:14:23.720
We are the product.

00:14:23.720 --> 00:14:25.500
If the fighters are not the product,

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:27.920
then go ahead and release
us from our contracts.

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:30.460
You'll do fine without us, clearly.

00:14:30.460 --> 00:14:33.480
It's the identity, and
rank, and popularity

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:36.080
of those fighters that
attract the eyeballs

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:37.330
and make the sport what it is.

00:14:37.330 --> 00:14:39.070
And if you can lock all those up

00:14:39.070 --> 00:14:41.243
and keep them from other promotions.

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:48.660
William Isaacson, a lawyer for UFC,

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:51.620
said in a statement that
UFC has spent many years

00:14:51.620 --> 00:14:53.960
building a world-class organization,

00:14:53.960 --> 00:14:56.230
enhancing the brand of our athletes,

00:14:56.230 --> 00:14:59.210
and championing the sport of MMA.

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:02.630
UFC pays its fighters more
than any other MMA promoter,

00:15:02.630 --> 00:15:04.890
with average fighter compensation rising

00:15:04.890 --> 00:15:08.360
by over 600% since 2005.

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:10.700
Our efforts have raised
the global popularity

00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:13.390
of the sport and the
overall revenue potential

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:16.840
of all MMA organizations and athletes.

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:21.400
In December of 2020,

00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:25.940
the judge announced a
crucial step in this lawsuit,

00:15:25.940 --> 00:15:30.750
which is that he is going to
certify it as a class-action.

00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:33.870
Class certification makes this lawsuit

00:15:33.870 --> 00:15:37.310
a much more significant threat to UFC.

00:15:37.310 --> 00:15:39.940
And the judge made clear long before

00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:41.660
saying how he planned to rule,

00:15:41.660 --> 00:15:44.630
that he expected whatever
he did to be appealed

00:15:44.630 --> 00:15:46.330
into Federal Appeals Court,

00:15:46.330 --> 00:15:49.080
and on from there to the US Supreme Court.

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:53.020
So we are likely in for many more months

00:15:53.020 --> 00:15:57.403
and years of wrangling over this.

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:04.320
Kyle Kingsbury said that he
doesn't think any settlement

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:07.150
would be acceptable unless it includes

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:11.630
a change to the restrictive
contracts that UFC uses

00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:15.760
to keep fighters bound to the UFC.

00:16:15.760 --> 00:16:17.370
My son's about to turn six years old

00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:19.180
and it started when he was in the womb.

00:16:19.180 --> 00:16:21.700
You know, we're in this thing to the end

00:16:21.700 --> 00:16:23.300
to change the sport forever.

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:25.960
We want the same freedoms that are offered

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:27.350
in any other major sport.

00:16:27.350 --> 00:16:29.590
We are, every other major
sport has gone through

00:16:29.590 --> 00:16:31.880
what we're going through right now.

00:16:31.880 --> 00:16:34.552
People tell fighters,
you know, just shut up,

00:16:34.552 --> 00:16:37.450
look at how lucky you are.

00:16:37.450 --> 00:16:40.743
Well no, actually, we're
fighting for what we believe in.

00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:43.940
We're gonna continue being fighters.

00:16:43.940 --> 00:16:45.903
And that's how we're made.

